const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter?', a: 'The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter is a free, browser-based tool that converts a number between any bases from 2 to 36 with arbitrary-precision arithmetic. It shows the common bases at once, detects 0x, 0b, and 0o prefixes, and includes a twos-complement mode with a selectable bit width. Nothing is uploaded.' },
  { q: 'How do you convert binary to decimal?', a: 'Multiply each binary digit by 2 raised to the power of its position, then add. So 100101 is 32 plus 4 plus 1, which is 37. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter does this instantly and shows the result in decimal, hex, and octal at the same time, so you do not have to work it out by hand.' },
  { q: 'Why is one hex digit four bits?', a: 'Because 16 is 2 to the fourth power, so each hexadecimal digit from 0 to F stands for exactly four binary digits. That makes hex a compact way to write bytes: 8 bits is two hex digits. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter shows the binary and hex forms side by side so the mapping is clear.' },
  { q: 'Why does a color code use hexadecimal?', a: 'Because each color channel is one byte, from 0 to 255, which is exactly two hex digits. A code like #C60018 sets red, green, and blue as three two-digit hex values. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter converts each channel between hex and decimal so you can read or build a color code.' },
  { q: 'What is twos complement?', a: 'It is how computers store signed integers in binary. The most significant bit carries a negative place value, so the pattern encodes negative numbers. Because of this, a negative value needs a fixed bit width. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter includes a twos-complement mode where you set that width.' },
  { q: 'Why does my negative number look different at 8 and 16 bits?', a: 'Because twos complement depends on the bit width. Minus one is 11111111 in 8 bits but 1111111111111111 in 16 bits. There is no single binary form for a negative number without a width. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter asks for the width so the answer is always correct.' },
  { q: 'How do I read a chmod number like 755?', a: 'It is octal, base 8. Each digit adds read (4), write (2), and execute (1) for the owner, group, and others. So 755 means rwxr-xr-x. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter converts between octal and binary, which is how permission bits are actually stored, so 755 becomes 111101101.' },
  { q: 'What bases does the converter support?', a: 'Any base from 2 to 36, using the digits 0 to 9 and then A to Z. That covers binary, octal, decimal, and hexadecimal, plus less common bases like base 32. Most free converters stop at hex, but the Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter handles the full range and shows the common ones together.' },
  { q: 'Can it handle very large numbers?', a: 'Yes. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter uses arbitrary-precision arithmetic, so numbers far beyond the usual 64-bit limit convert exactly with no rounding or overflow. This matters for cryptographic values, large hex dumps, and any case where a standard calculator would silently lose digits.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between signed and unsigned?', a: 'An unsigned number is always zero or positive and uses every bit for magnitude. A signed number uses twos complement, so the top bit marks it negative, which halves the positive range. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter lets you switch between the two, so the same bits can be read either way.' },
  { q: 'Why is my binary number rejected?', a: 'Usually because it contains a digit that is not valid for base 2, which only allows 0 and 1. A stray 2 or 9 is not binary. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter flags digits that are invalid for the chosen base rather than producing a wrong answer, so you catch the typo immediately.' },
  { q: 'Do I need to add a 0x or 0b prefix?', a: 'You can, and the Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter auto-detects 0x for hex, 0b for binary, and 0o for octal. You can also just pick the base from the menu and type the digits. Either way, the converter shows the value across the other bases at the same time.' },
  { q: 'Is my data uploaded to a server?', a: 'No. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter runs entirely in your browser, so the numbers you convert never leave your device. There is no signup and no server-side processing, which is consistent with how every Best Answer Hub tool is built. The conversion is exact arithmetic done locally.' },
  { q: 'Does it work offline?', a: 'Yes. Once the Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter page has loaded, it works with no internet connection, because base conversion is deterministic arithmetic that needs no external data. That makes it reliable on a locked-down machine or anywhere a connection is not available.' },
  { q: 'Is the Number Base Converter free?', a: 'Yes. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter is completely free with no usage limits, no watermarks, and no signup. It stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising, so it carries no ads and never gates a conversion behind an upgrade.' },
];

const seo = {
  title: 'Binary, Hex, and Octal: How Base Conversion Works',
  description: 'How number bases convert, why one hex digit is four bits, and the twos-complement bit-width trap that makes a negative number look different at 8 and 16 bits.',
  url: '/playbooks/number-base-converter/',
