        <div class="cav">Same number, many faces</div>
        <h1>Binary, Hex, and Octal: <em>How Base Conversion Works</em></h1>
        <p class="dek">The number 37 can be written as 100101, 45, 37, or 25 depending on the base. Converting between them is simple arithmetic until negative numbers show up, where the answer depends on how many bits you are using. Here is how base conversion works, why one hex digit is exactly four bits, and the two's-complement trap that catches people out.</p>
        <div class="byline"><span><b>Best Answer Hub</b></span><span>~8 min read</span><span>Developer tools</span><span>No signup</span></div>
        <div class="bandstrip">
          <div class="seg b1">Convert<small>any base, 2 to 36</small></div>
          <div class="seg b2">Understand<small>hex, binary, octal</small></div>
          <div class="seg b3">Signed<small>two's complement</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="stat-grid">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">4</div><div class="l">bits in one hexadecimal digit</div><div class="src">Princeton COS 217</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">2 to 36</div><div class="l">bases this converter handles</div><div class="src">Best Answer Hub</div></div>
