      <div class="stat"><div class="n">755</div><div class="l">the octal chmod for rwxr-xr-x</div><div class="src">Linux man page</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="layout">
      <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <div class="t">On this page</div>
        <a href="#what">What it is</a>
        <a href="#how">How conversion works</a>
        <a href="#hex">Why hex exists</a>
        <a href="#twos">The two's complement trap</a>
        <a href="#uses">Where you use each base</a>
        <a href="#different">Why this tool</a>
        <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
      </nav>

      <article>
        <p class="lead"><strong>Number base conversion is positional arithmetic: each digit is worth the base raised to the power of its position, so the same value can be written in binary, octal, decimal, or hexadecimal without changing what it is.</strong> The one place it gets subtle is negative numbers, where the binary form depends on the bit width you choose. The Best Answer Hub <a href="/calculators/stem-suite/number-base-converter/">Number Base Converter</a> converts across bases 2 to 36 at once, handles two's complement with an explicit bit width, and runs in your browser.</p>

        <p>This guide covers how base conversion actually works, why hexadecimal is the compact shorthand for binary, the two's-complement bit-width trap, and where each base turns up in real work. The tool sits in the Best Answer Hub <a href="/developer-toolbox/">Developer Toolbox</a> and its STEM calculator suite, is built and maintained by <a href="/about/shahbaz-ali-malik/">Shahbaz Ali Malik</a>, and stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising.</p>

        <div class="cav">Start here</div>
        <h2 id="what">What is the Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter?</h2>
        <p>The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter is a free, browser-based tool that converts a number between any bases from 2 to 36, using arbitrary-precision arithmetic so large values stay exact. It shows all the common bases at once, auto-detects the <code>0x</code>, <code>0b</code>, and <code>0o</code> prefixes, and includes a two's-complement mode with a selectable bit width for signed numbers. Everything runs in your browser with no signup and nothing uploaded.</p>
        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">1</span><div><b>Any base, all at once.</b> Convert across bases 2 to 36 and see binary, octal, decimal, and hex together, not one at a time.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">2</span><div><b>Signed numbers done right.</b> A two's-complement mode with an explicit bit width, so a negative value is never ambiguous.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">3</span><div><b>Exact at any size.</b> Arbitrary-precision arithmetic means very large numbers convert without rounding.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">The mechanics</div>
        <h2 id="how">How does base conversion work?</h2>
        <p>Every number is a sum of its digits, each multiplied by the base raised to the power of its position. In base 2, the number 100101 is 1 times 32, plus 1 times 4, plus 1 times 1, which is 37 (<a href="https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr19/cos217/lectures/05_NumberSystems.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Princeton COS 217</a>). To go the other way, from decimal to another base, you divide repeatedly by the target base and read the remainders from bottom to top. The four common bases just use different digit sets.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Base</th><th>Name</th><th>Digits</th><th>37 looks like</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">2</td><td>Binary</td><td>0-1</td><td><code>100101</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">8</td><td>Octal</td><td>0-7</td><td><code>45</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">10</td><td>Decimal</td><td>0-9</td><td><code>37</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">16</td><td>Hexadecimal</td><td>0-9, A-F</td><td><code>25</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="codebox">
          <div class="dt">Positional notation, worked out</div>
          <div class="codelines">100101 (base 2)
  = 1x32 + 0x16 + 0x8 + 1x4 + 0x2 + 1x1
  = 32 + 4 + 1
  = 37 (base 10)</div>
          <p class="codenote">The same method works for any base: multiply each digit by the base to the power of its position, then add. Source: Princeton COS 217.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">The compact one</div>
        <h2 id="hex">Why does hexadecimal exist?</h2>
        <p>Because one hex digit maps exactly to four binary digits, making hex a tidy shorthand for binary. Since 16 is 2 to the fourth power, every hex digit from 0 to F stands for a group of four bits, so a byte (8 bits) is just two hex digits (<a href="https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr19/cos217/lectures/05_NumberSystems.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Princeton COS 217</a>). That is why hex shows up wherever raw bytes do, including CSS colors, where each of the red, green, and blue channels is a two-digit hex value from 00 to ff (<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/hex-color" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MDN</a>).</p>
        <figure class="chart">
          <figcaption class="chart-title">One hex digit equals four bits</figcaption>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 600 150" role="img" aria-label="The hex value C6 maps to the binary 1100 0110, which is 198 in decimal. Each hex digit becomes exactly four bits.">
            <rect x="20" y="45" width="120" height="60" rx="10" fill="#C6603F"/>
            <text x="80" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="22" font-weight="800" fill="#fff">C6</text>
            <text x="80" y="92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#f3ddd2">hexadecimal</text>
            <path d="M144 75 L184 75" stroke="#C6603F" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M178 69 l8 6 -8 6" fill="none" stroke="#C6603F" stroke-width="2.5"/>
            <rect x="190" y="45" width="105" height="60" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#E8DECE" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="242" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#8a4429">1100</text>
            <text x="242" y="93" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#847B6B">C = 12</text>
            <rect x="300" y="45" width="105" height="60" rx="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#E8DECE" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="352" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace, monospace" font-size="17" font-weight="700" fill="#8a4429">0110</text>
            <text x="352" y="93" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#847B6B">6 = 6</text>
            <path d="M409 75 L449 75" stroke="#C6603F" stroke-width="2.5"/><path d="M443 69 l8 6 -8 6" fill="none" stroke="#C6603F" stroke-width="2.5"/>
            <rect x="455" y="45" width="125" height="60" rx="10" fill="#E4EADD" stroke="#cdd9c8" stroke-width="2"/>
            <text x="517" y="72" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Bricolage Grotesque, sans-serif" font-size="22" font-weight="800" fill="#33302A">198</text>
            <text x="517" y="92" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Karla, sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#5a7355">decimal</text>
          </svg>
          <p class="chart-src">Each hex digit expands to exactly four bits (C = 1100, 6 = 0110), so C6 is 11000110, which is 198. Source: Princeton COS 217; MDN.</p>
        </figure>

        <div class="cav">The gotcha</div>
        <h2 id="twos">The two's complement trap: why bit width matters</h2>
        <p>A negative number has no single binary form: it depends on how many bits you are using. Computers store signed integers in two's complement, where the top bit carries a negative place value, so the same value fills a different pattern at 8, 16, or 32 bits. As one university course puts it, "when we describe the representation of a negative binary number, we need to say how many bits are in the representation," noting that minus one is 11111111 in 8 bits but 1111111111111111 in 16 bits (<a href="https://uo-cis211.github.io/chapters/06_2_NegBits.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Oregon, CIS 211</a>).</p>
        <div class="codebox">
          <div class="dt">The same negative, at different widths</div>
          <div class="codelines">-1 as  8-bit twos complement  =  11111111
-1 as 16-bit twos complement  =  11111111 11111111

-8 as  4-bit twos complement  =  1000   (the lowest 4-bit value)
+7 as  4-bit twos complement  =  0111   (the highest 4-bit value)</div>
          <p class="codenote">A converter that ignores bit width cannot answer "what is -1 in binary" correctly. The Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter asks for the width. Source: University of Oregon, CIS 211.</p>
        </div>
        <p>This is the single most common base-conversion mistake, and it is why the Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter makes you pick a bit width for signed values instead of guessing. It also flags digits that are invalid for a base, so a stray 2 in a binary number does not silently produce a wrong answer.</p>

        <div class="cav">In the wild</div>
        <h2 id="uses">Where do you actually use each base?</h2>
        <p>Each base earns its keep in a different corner of computing. Hexadecimal writes colors and bytes, octal sets file permissions, and binary underlies logic and networking. If you have ever run <code>chmod 755</code>, you have used octal: each digit is read (4) plus write (2) plus execute (1) for owner, group, and others, so 755 means <code>rwxr-xr-x</code> (<a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Linux man page</a>).</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Base</th><th>Where it shows up</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="band">Binary</td><td>Digital logic, bitmasks, IP subnetting</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Octal</td><td>Unix file permissions (chmod 755)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="band">Hexadecimal</td><td>CSS colors, memory addresses, byte dumps</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>Because these come up constantly and are easy to get slightly wrong, a converter that shows every base at once and is strict about bit width and valid digits saves real debugging time.</p>

        <div class="cav">Why this one</div>
        <h2 id="different">How is Best Answer Hub different from other converters?</h2>
        <p>The difference is range and rigor. Many free converters only do binary, octal, decimal, and hex; the Best Answer Hub Number Base Converter handles any base from 2 to 36, shows them together, and treats signed numbers properly with an explicit bit width. It uses arbitrary-precision arithmetic so large values do not lose accuracy, and it runs entirely in your browser.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>What you get</th><th>Best Answer Hub</th><th>Typical converter</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bases supported</td><td class="band">2 to 36</td><td>Often just 2, 8, 10, 16</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Two's complement with bit width</td><td class="band">Yes, explicit</td><td>Often missing</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Large-number precision</td><td class="band">Arbitrary precision</td><td>Can overflow</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Signup or ads</td><td class="band">None, in browser</td><td>Varies</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="cta">
          <span class="kicker">Convert across every base</span>
          <h3>Try the free Number Base Converter</h3>
          <p>Binary, octal, decimal, hex, and any base to 36, with two's complement and arbitrary precision. No signup, in your browser.</p>
          <a class="btn" href="/calculators/stem-suite/number-base-converter/">Open the Number Base Converter</a>
        </div>

        <div class="cav">Good questions</div>
        <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions about number bases</h2>
        <div class="faq">
          {faqs.map((f) => (
            <div class="qa">
              <div class="q" set:html={f.q}></div>
              <div class="a" set:html={f.a}></div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <div class="cav">People also read</div>
        <h2>Keep going</h2>
        <div class="sec">
          <ul class="pillars">
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/hash-generator/">Generate a Hash (SHA-256), Nothing Uploaded</a></b> Another low-level explainer, done in the browser.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/molecular-weight-calculator/">How to Calculate the Molar Mass of Any Formula</a></b> The other STEM-suite tool, for chemistry.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/playbooks/free-developer-tools/">Free Developer Tools That Run in Your Browser</a></b> The Developer Toolbox guide, all keeping your data local.</div></li>
            <li><span class="dot">&rarr;</span><div><b><a href="/knowledge-center/smb-ai-readiness/">SMB AI Readiness Score</a></b> A free assessment with an instant radar and quick wins for your business.</div></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>Sources</h2>
        <ul class="sources">
          <li>Princeton University, <a href="https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/spr19/cos217/lectures/05_NumberSystems.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">COS 217, Number Systems</a>, 2019 (positional notation; 1 hex digit = 4 bits).</li>
          <li>MDN Web Docs, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/hex-color" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">hex-color</a>, 2026 (each color channel is two hex digits, 00 to ff).</li>
          <li>University of Oregon, <a href="https://uo-cis211.github.io/chapters/06_2_NegBits.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CIS 211, Negative Binary Numbers</a>, 2020 (two's complement needs a bit width).</li>
          <li>Linux man pages, <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">chmod(1)</a>, 2025 (octal file permissions; 4, 2, 1 bits).</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-bae-border-light text-sm text-bae-muted">More free developer tools: <a href="/developer-toolbox/" class="text-bae-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-bae-primary transition-colors">Developer Toolbox</a>, <a href="/tools/hash-generator/" class="text-bae-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-bae-primary transition-colors">Hash Generator</a>, <a href="/tools/color-palette-generator/" class="text-bae-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-bae-primary transition-colors">Color Palette Generator</a>, and <a href="/tools/" class="text-bae-ink underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-bae-primary transition-colors">all free tools</a>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
